<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiajing Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1507">1507</a>–1567</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Zhu Houcong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jiajing Emperor, personal name Zhu Houcong, was the 12th emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, reigning from 1521 to 1567. A cousin of the Zhengde Emperor, who died without an heir, he came to the throne unexpectedly and at once clashed with his officials in the Great Rites Controversy, from which he emerged victorious after three years. From 1542 he lived in the West Park outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> among Taoist temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and trusted advisers, and his pursuit of immortality led him to elixirs containing arsenic, lead and mercury. His reign saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>'s raids, which reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1550, and wokou piracy that peaked in the 1550s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor.jpg" alt="Jiajing Emperor" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_officiel_de_l'empereur_Jiajing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early Ming emperors favoured Taoism and gave its practitioners many posts in the state’s ritual offices, and the <b>Jiajing Emperor</b> (r. 1521–1567) persecuted Buddhism at court in favour of Taoism.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhongxiang City, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.17,112.59&amp;year=1546" aria-label="Zhongxiang City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7459 1028 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jiajing Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7909 1298h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7909 1298h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.17,112.59&amp;year=1546">What was here in 1546? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
